      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2919.3,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 618,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two types of atoms: hydrogen and oxygen. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**—meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHowever, to humans, Earth, and life itself, water is much more than a simple chemical formula. Here is a breakdown of what water is through different lenses:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical Properties (The Science)\nWater is famous for being utterly unique among common substances on Earth:\n* **The Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states: **liquid** (oceans, rain), **solid** (ice, snow), and **gas** (steam, vapor).\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure, water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it carries vital nutrients in our bodies and minerals through the earth.\n* **Density Anomaly:** Most substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water actually *expands* and becomes less dense when it turns to ice. This is why ice floats, which prevents lakes and oceans from freezing solid from the bottom up, allowing marine life to survive the winter.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb and release large amounts of heat without drastically changing its own temperature. This moderates Earth’s climate and keeps coastal areas from experiencing extreme temperature swings.\n\n### 2. Ecological Role (The Planet)\nEarth is often called the **\"Blue Planet\"** because water covers about **71% of its surface**. \n* **Distribution:** About 96.5% of Earth's water is salt water in the oceans. Of the remaining 3.5% that is fresh water, most is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible freshwater in lakes, rivers, and the atmosphere.\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water is in constant motion. Driven by the sun, it evaporates from the earth, forms clouds, falls back down as precipitation (rain or snow), and flows back to the oceans in an endless loop.\n\n### 3. Biological Role (Life)\nLife as we know it cannot exist without water. \n* **The Medium of Life:** The first living organisms evolved in Earth's oceans, and cellular processes inside all living things take place in a watery environment.\n* **Human Biology:** The human body is made up of roughly 60% water. It regulates our internal temperature, lubricates joints, flushes out waste, and delivers oxygen and nutrients to our cells. You can survive weeks without food, but only a few days without water.\n\n### Summary\nWater is the lifeblood of our planet. It shapes our geography, drives our weather, makes up our bodies, and connects every living ecosystem on Earth."
